      {
        "id": "rem-td-nonsmooth-phase-scope",
        "kind": "remark",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Scope at phase boundaries and in the thermodynamic limit",
        "reference": "items/rem-td-nonsmooth-phase-scope.md",
        "design_state": "Step 1 complete draft; readiness recorded separately.",
        "dependency_level": 11,
        "deps": [
          "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain",
          "def-td-thermodynamic-limit-contract",
          "pthm-td-clapeyron-local",
          "pthm-td-compact-continuous-phase-mixture"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-thermodynamic-limit-contract": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "pthm-td-clapeyron-local": "physical-result",
          "pthm-td-compact-continuous-phase-mixture": "physical-result"
        },
        "physical_scope": "Interpretive limits for the phase-branch, coexistence, Clapeyron, and compact-mixture statements on this page; this remark establishes no universal critical or thermodynamic-limit result.",
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "The branch theorems on this page require the stated smooth local charts. At a phase boundary, a composition boundary, or a critical point, the branch derivatives may fail to exist or may cease to distinguish the phases. In particular, [[pthm-td-clapeyron-local]] requires a nonzero molar-volume jump; when that jump vanishes, its local graph argument and quotient formula do not apply.\n\nThe compact-mixture result [[pthm-td-compact-continuous-phase-mixture]] requires a compact phase-generator domain, a continuous entropy map, a fixed barycenter, and feasibility of all probability mixtures with that barycenter. It supplies no attainment theorem for noncompact domains. Likewise, [[def-td-thermodynamic-limit-contract]] specifies the sequence and normalization for a limiting claim but proves no limit exists and no derivatives commute with that limit.\n\nA finite-state partition sum is finite and positive at each finite system size. A singular phase behavior therefore needs a separately specified limit or another model with a separately established domain and regularity analysis. This page does not assert a universal thermodynamic-limit theorem, ensemble equivalence, critical exponent, or physical applicability of the van der Waals equation.\n\nThe local homogeneity used by [[pthm-td-clapeyron-local]] may be independently stipulated in a phase model or supplied on the separately designated common-law bulk branch under the local Euler theorem's inverse-chart and common-reference conditions. A constructed local Gibbs model need not be embedded in a global bulk cone to use the general conditional result. The added branch supplies no universal finite-size, long-range or gravitational extensivity and does not remove any smoothness, exchange or nonzero-volume-jump condition.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, author-hosted complete lecture notes, §§5.1–5.4",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S5"
            },
            {
              "title": "Konstantin K. Likharev, Essential Graduate Physics: Statistical Mechanics, §§4.1–4.2",
              "url": "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Thermodynamics_and_Statistical_Mechanics/Essential_Graduate_Physics_-_Statistical_Mechanics_%28Likharev%29/04%3A_Phase_Transitions/4.01%3A_First_order_phase_transitions"
            }
          ]
        },
        "library": "physics"
      }
    ]
  },
