        },
        "physical_scope": "A constructed pair of smooth one-component Gibbs charts with zero molar-volume jump; it shows why the local nonzero-jump hypothesis cannot be omitted and makes no material claim.",
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Fix $T_0>0$, $p_0>0$, $g_0\\in\\mathbb R$, $v_0,c,\\kappa>0$, and distinct positive molar entropies $s_\\alpha,s_\\beta$. Use units $g_0\\in\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$, $v_0\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, $c\\in\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, and $\\kappa\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}Pa^{-1}}$. On a common open chart define\n\\[\ng_i(T,p)=g_0-s_i(T-T_0)-\\frac{c}{2T_0}(T-T_0)^2+v_0(p-p_0)-\\frac{\\kappa}{2}(p-p_0)^2,\n\\]\nwith $i\\in\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}$. Let $G_i=n_i g_i$ and adopt $S_i=-\\partial_TG_i$, $V_i=\\partial_pG_i$, $\\mu_i=\\partial_{n_i}G_i$, and $U_i=G_i+TS_i-pV_i$. This gives same-tag scaling and smooth local Gibbs charts. At $(T_0,p_0)$ the molar Gibbs values agree and the molar-volume jump is zero; the entropies differ.",
