        "justified_by": [],
        "forward_refs": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-phase-and-coexistence-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "pthm-td-regular-gibbs-phase-rule": "physical-result",
          "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "physical_scope": "A constructed smooth local two-phase Gibbs model with one formal internal reaction, used to verify the local equation-map nonsingular-minor hypothesis only; no stable or realized coexistence claim, empirical system, or chemical data are made.",
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Take $C=3$ species and $P=2$ phases. Write $x_3:=1-x_1-x_2$, and center the open composition charts at $x_1=x_2=x_3=1/3$. Fix $T_0>0$, $p_0>0$, $s_0,c,v_0,\\kappa,q>0$, and $\\eta=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. The parameters have units $s_0,c\\in\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$, $v_0\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$, $\\kappa\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}Pa^{-1}}$, and $q\\in\\mathrm{m^3\\,mol^{-1}}$. For $\\gamma\\in\\{\\alpha,\\beta\\}$, set $a_\\alpha=2\\eta$, $a_\\beta=\\eta$, $q_\\alpha=0$, and $q_\\beta=q$, and define molar Gibbs potentials\n\\[\ng^\\gamma(T,p,x)=-s_0(T-T_0)-\\frac{c}{2T_0}(T-T_0)^2+v_0(p-p_0)-\\frac{\\kappa}{2}(p-p_0)^2+\\frac{a_\\gamma}{2}(x_1^2+x_2^2)-\\frac{a_\\gamma}{3}(x_1+x_2)+\\frac{a_\\gamma}{9}+q_\\gamma(p-p_0)x_3.\n\\]\nSet $G^\\gamma=m_\\gamma g^\\gamma$ on each fixed-tag branch and adopt the natural Gibbs assignments $S=-G_T$, $V=G_p$, and $\\mu_j=G_{n_j}$, with $U=G+TS-pV$. These smooth charts are scale-invariant in amount. Near the centered point the volumes are positive, and the $(U,V)$ chart Jacobian at fixed composition and amount is nonsingular: $U_T=m_\\gamma c$, $V_p=-m_\\gamma\\kappa$, and $V_T=0$ there, so its determinant is $-m_\\gamma^2c\\kappa\\ne0$. The reaction vector is $\\nu=(-1,-1/2,1)^{\\mathsf T}$, of rank $R=1$. At $(T_0,p_0,x_1,x_2)=(T_0,p_0,1/3,1/3)$, both phases have $\\mu^\\gamma=(0,0,0)$, so the phase-equality equations and reaction affinity vanish.",
